select-case refactor -- If ladders into pattern matching
=========================================================

THE CHANGE
----------
Nested If/Else ladders three or more decisions deep read badly: every
rejection buries the interesting arm one indent further right.  Convert
them to Select Case, which is an expression like everything else -- each
clause's block yields the value.

There are two shapes, and picking the right one is most of the job.

1.  Dispatch.  The chain tests ONE value against several candidates:

        If want = ObjNamed("BIRD") Then ...
        Else If want = ObjNamed("SNAKE") Then ...

    becomes

        Select Case want
            Case ObjNamed("BIRD")
                ...
            Case ObjNamed("SNAKE")
                ...
            Case Else
                ...

    Clause expressions may be literals, locals, or function calls, and
    combine as any-of lists (Case a, b, c).  All compare by equality.
    A name that is a declared Type is always a destructuring pattern;
    everything else is an expression.  Bare non-local names do evaluate
    in clause position, but write the parentheses (Case Lamp(), not
    Case Lamp) so nobody has to know that.

2.  Guard ladder.  The chain tests UNRELATED conditions in sequence:

        Select Case True
            Case dest = NoRoom()
                False
            Case Not InDeep(dest)
                False
            ...
            Case Else
                True

    Clause tests evaluate lazily, in order, stopping at the first match
    (the construct desugars to nested Ifte), so a clause may rely on
    every clause above it having failed -- exactly like the If ladder it
    replaces.  Note this idiom is not in the language docs; it follows
    from clause-expression equality plus boolean =, and holds today.

Either way: preserve clause order EXACTLY.  In the nested form a guard
relationship is structural -- you cannot reach the InDeep test without
passing the NoRoom test that keeps it in range.  In the flat form it is
only convention, and a reorder compiles clean and diverges quietly.
Always write Case Else: a non-match without one aborts at runtime,
which turns a "can't happen" arm into a crash when it happens.  An arm
with a sub-decision keeps a nested If inside its Case body; a Let
inside a Case body is legal.

WHEN TO AVOID IT
----------------
  - Two arms.  If/Else with one condition is already the minimal form;
    Select Case True adds two lines and an indent for nothing.

  - Disguised disjunctions.  A ladder whose arms mostly return True or
    False is one boolean: a Or b Or c.  And/Or short-circuit in
    expression bodies, so the rewrite is safe -- but keep it on one
    line or inside parentheses.  A wrapped And/Or continuation line
    outside brackets grabs a stray stack value instead of erroring.

  - Recursive walkers.  Base-case guard, then Lets, then the step is
    the recursion idiom; leave it alone.

  - Mid-chain Lets.  A Let between two tests must hoist above the
    Select or duplicate into arms.  Hoist only what is pure and cheap,
    and never move anything touching Rnd, Input, or Print -- changing
    when an effect or a draw happens changes the program.

  - Compound guards on the scrutinee.  Where attaches only to Type
    patterns, so "want = vase And prop = 0" cannot be a guarded
    equality Case.  Under three levels deep, keep the If.

HOW TO TEST IT
--------------
The refactor must preserve results AND the order and count of effects
and random draws.  Laziness makes that hold clause-for-clause; your job
is to not reorder and not hoist effects.

  - Syntax in doubt?  Probe it: a five-line mill in a scratch
    directory, then "bin/mill run scratch.shoddy".  (Build the mill
    first if needed: dotnet publish src/Shoddy.Mill -c Release -o bin.)
    Clause laziness, call-expression clauses, bare locals, and Let in a
    Case body were all confirmed this way; re-probe after language
    changes, since the True ladder leans on undocumented behavior.

  - Language-level regressions: dotnet test src/Shoddy.Tests runs the
    golden conformance suite.

  - Mill-level: run the mill's own harness if it has one, and trust it
    over your eyes.  open-cave is the exacting case: its recorded
    transcripts are the specification, and 92 of them pin the RNG seed,
    so one extra or missing draw diverges every seeded game.  There,
    run tests/run.sh --smoke (~3 min) between edits and the full
    tests/run.sh (~18 min) before calling the change finished.  The
    sweep recompiles sources per transcript -- finish editing before
    you start it, or it certifies a moving target.

  - No harness?  Play the mill before and after along the paths the
    function serves, and diff the sessions.
